WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.252
List what hello-devops needs by now.

2
00:00:05.252 --> 00:00:11.844
A Dockerfile, two workflows with pinned actions, Kubernetes YAML, and an Argo CD Application.

3
00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:15.631
A ServiceMonitor, a dashboard, log shipping and tracing.

4
00:00:15.631 --> 00:00:17.859
Policies, and a way to get secrets.

5
00:00:17.859 --> 00:00:20.215
You learned all of it in this course.

6
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:24.195
Now picture 40 product teams, each building that alone.

7
00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:28.998
They solve the same problems 40 times, in 40 slightly different ways.

8
00:00:28.998 --> 00:00:32.041
And whoever is on call has to learn every one.

9
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.862
Most large companies answer this with platform engineering.

10
00:00:36.862 --> 00:00:46.027
A platform team builds an internal developer platform: the self-service capabilities that product teams use to build, ship and run software.

11
00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:48.032
Self-service is the centre.

12
00:00:48.032 --> 00:00:54.759
A developer gets an environment, a database or a pipeline without filing a ticket and waiting a week.

13
00:00:54.967 --> 00:00:59.109
A classic operations team does work for other teams, through tickets.

14
00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:03.791
A platform team builds things, so that other teams can do the work themselves.

15
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.329
It runs the platform as a product, with developers as its customers.

16
00:01:08.329 --> 00:01:13.549
It measures success by adoption and speed, never by the number of tools installed.

17
00:01:14.467 --> 00:01:20.251
The platform's main product is the golden path, which some companies call the paved road.

18
00:01:20.251 --> 00:01:23.696
It is a supported, ready-made way to do a common job.

19
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.480
The classic one is create a new service.

20
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.415
You fill in a name and a team.

21
00:01:28.633 --> 00:01:39.051
You get a repository with a Dockerfile, CI, a release workflow, Kubernetes YAML, an Argo CD Application and a dashboard, already wired together.

22
00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:41.321
Each path makes two promises.

23
00:01:41.321 --> 00:01:47.131
Compliant: security and policy are built in, so the easy way is also the safe way.

24
00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:53.143
Tested: the platform team keeps the template working, so a new service starts with current versions.

25
00:01:53.967 --> 00:01:57.498
Developers find these paths through a developer portal.

26
00:01:57.498 --> 00:02:04.497
Backstage is the best-known framework for building one, with a software catalog, templates, docs and plugins.

27
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.087
But Backstage is only the shop front.

28
00:02:07.087 --> 00:02:11.860
The platform is the shop behind it: the templates, pipelines and clusters.

29
00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:14.902
The guardrails come with the path.

30
00:02:14.902 --> 00:02:24.435
A new service starts with pinned actions, a signed image with an SBOM, its own ServiceAccount, the Kyverno policies, and a dashboard with alerts.

31
00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:28.129
Teams may still leave the path, when they have a good reason.

32
00:02:28.129 --> 00:02:33.917
But the cluster enforces the same rules for everyone, so a team that leaves the path still meets them.

33
00:02:34.133 --> 00:02:37.405
And if many teams leave the path, the path is wrong.

34
00:02:37.405 --> 00:02:40.803
That is the most useful feedback a platform team gets.

35
00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:44.897
One guardrail matters for money: the team label.

36
00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:52.364
In the cloud, any engineer can spend money with one command, and the bill arrives a month later, with no names on it.

37
00:02:52.567 --> 00:02:58.531
FinOps makes that cost visible, and shared between engineering, finance and the business.

38
00:02:58.531 --> 00:03:02.351
It loops through three phases: inform, optimize, operate.

39
00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:04.795
Everything starts with allocation.

40
00:03:04.795 --> 00:03:08.137
Until a cost has an owner, every saving is a guess.

41
00:03:08.137 --> 00:03:11.611
In Kubernetes, the owner is a label on each workload.

42
00:03:12.533 --> 00:03:20.797
A cost tool gives each Pod a share of its node's price, based on the CPU and memory it requests, or uses if that is more.

43
00:03:20.797 --> 00:03:23.256
Then it adds up the shares by label.

44
00:03:23.467 --> 00:03:27.350
Capacity that nobody requested still appears on the bill.

45
00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:28.508
It shows as idle.

46
00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:32.255
Give that line an owner too, usually the platform team.

47
00:03:32.467 --> 00:03:37.838
Then each team gets a report of what it used, while the money stays in a central budget.

48
00:03:37.838 --> 00:03:40.096
That is showback, and it comes first.

49
00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:44.787
Teams change what they run, once they see the numbers next to their own name.

50
00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.769
Totals grow when the business grows, so a unit cost works better.

51
00:03:49.769 --> 00:03:54.652
If traffic doubles and the bill grows by 40 percent, each request got cheaper.

52
00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:57.246
hello-devops already has both inputs.

53
00:03:57.246 --> 00:04:01.941
Prometheus counts its requests, and the team label says whose cost it is.

54
00:04:02.867 --> 00:04:03.948
Put it together.

55
00:04:03.948 --> 00:04:07.464
The template creates a repository with a team label.

56
00:04:07.464 --> 00:04:09.018
CI and policy check it.

57
00:04:09.018 --> 00:04:12.534
It runs on the cluster, which reserves its requests.

58
00:04:12.534 --> 00:04:15.103
And showback shows the cost per owner.

59
00:04:15.333 --> 00:04:19.777
Cost reports, and teams that leave the path, feed back into the template.

60
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:29.500
A platform team turns repeated work into a self-service golden path, with the guardrails built in, and a team label that gives every cost an owner.
